A||||3||GWMORGAN||Droaning From The Rear-end.||||9
Z||000000||GWMORGAN||10-29-1999||12:32 AM||||Ever since being hit (sideways) on the right rear tire by a motorist, I'm getting a droaning sound which fills the cockpit when I'm making right corners at highway speed. The inner and outer tie-rods were replaced (also realigned) but not the wheel bearing. Could it be the bearing or CV joint gone bad? How hard is it to replace both...do I need any special tools. Thanks for info.<BR>                    86 GT.||||reg||9
Z||000001||dogtired||10-29-1999||08:03 AM||||On my 87SE I thought some of the same things you are suspecting but it turned out to be the guard behind the brake was slightly bent and if you would go arround a corner just right there was that noise. Pulled rear wheel off bent the tin back just 1/2 inch and noise was gone. Just a quick look-see might save you some bucks$$||||reg||3
Z||000002||theogre||10-29-1999||09:34 AM||||a grinding/scraping noise durring cornering could easily be the stone sheild alright. Doesn't hurt to try it.<P>If you took a direct hit to the wheel tho you're right in thinking you have come damage. The damage most likely is dimples in the side face of the ball bearing races. when you turn the hub pushes the bearings against the dimples. this would make sort of a rumbling sound. when your not turning the bearing runs in good race and is quiet.<P>I would not drive much until you replace the hub. those dimples can eat up the bearings fairly quickly. You should not need a new half shaft.<P>To replace the hub you need a 18mm, i think, socket, and a axle nut wrench. 30mm I think. I'll try to remeber check it latter. You should also replace the axle nut. these are a lock nut and intended to be use only once. they are only a couple dollars. <P>You need a 1/2 inch drive ratchet or bar for the axle nut. probably a pipe to. You can take it off with a impact wrench. Do NOT put it on with one.<P>you'll also need a seal driver or pipe for the new half shaft seal. You drive it thru from the wheel side till it just starts to pop out the back. Lightly grease the opening in the casting to make it a bit easier to drive it straight. grease the seal lip before inserting the half shaft. <P>If you are still running the struck rim then have it checked for damage too. it's likely bent. you can do that on a spin balancer real quick.||||reg||1
Z||000003||GWMORGAN||10-29-1999||11:55 AM||||Hey Orge...You were right!!The bearing was shot.Did the fix myself, now she runs perfect<BR>I am designating you "Chairman of The Board" because 'YOU DA MAN'||||reg||1
